186411406 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411406 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f061e5ea-3298-55a4-9377-e3f6be2f4109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411407 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411407 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411407 #edb/ref 1077 "field-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411407 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411407 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa977fd4-3715-5eee-bc40-a52f0c7898ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411408 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411408 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411408 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411408 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fecaf988-cb99-5dc9-a94d-0ddb5fc7e352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411409 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411409 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411409 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A={1,2,3}$ and consider the relation \"is smaller than\" $(<)$ on $A.$ If this relation is given by the set $R,$ express $R$ as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(1,2),(1,$\n{{field-1}}$),($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411409 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d410e49-d4f5-55b7-a3f6-063935c55bfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411409 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x<y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=3$ satisfy $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,2),(1,3).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,3).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then there is no value of  $y\\in A$ that satisfies $x<y.$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(1,2),(1,3),(2,3)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411410 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411410 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411410 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411410 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={{0},{0,1}}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nX\\,R\\,Y\\,⇔\\,X⊃Y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0}\\,R\\,{0,1}$\n- ${0,1}\\,R\\,{0,1}$\n- ${0}\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,{0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411410 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2006d399-9a69-519f-8bb4-45f612aefe6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411410 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊂{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $X\\,R\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊃Y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $⊃$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $⊅$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊃,$ we have ${0}⊃{0,1}.$ This is equivalent to ${0,1}⊂{0},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊃,$ we have ${0,1}⊃{0,1}.$ This is equivalent to ${0,1}⊂{0,1},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $⊅,$ we have ${0}⊅{0}.$ This is equivalent to ${0}⊄{0},$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411411 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411411 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411411 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411411 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A={-1,1}$ and consider the relation \"is not equal to\" $(\\ne )$ on $A.$ Which of the following sets describes the relation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411411 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27a0c14e-ebcc-5c2f-8221-848aff0bd39b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411411 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x\\ne y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=-1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x\\ne y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=-1$ satisfies $x\\ne y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(-1,1),(1,-1)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411412 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411412 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411412 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411412 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B={2,3,4}.$ Whi