scribe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(5,5),(7,7),(8,8)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411415 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411415 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,3}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $1\\,R\\,1$\n- $1\\,R\\,3$\n- $3\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fbac889-73d0-5b8f-a301-da4ef8cb327a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411415 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x=y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $=$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\ne $ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is  true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=$, we have $1=1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $1=3,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $3\\ne 3,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411416 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411416 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411416 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={2,4,6}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∤y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $2\\,R\\,4$\n- $4\\,R\\,6$\n- $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411416 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "77fae439-40b7-5f50-94ed-61df5583b377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411416 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∤y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∤$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∣$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤$, we have $2∤4,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $4∤6,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $2∣6,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411417 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411417 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85997" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411417 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411417 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A={1,2,3}$ and consider the relation \"is equal to\" $(=)$ on $A.$ Which of the following sets describes the relation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411417 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "838da398-ab9b-51bd-b33e-d5677f36abd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411417 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x=y.$\n\nLet's check each element $