 of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x⊈y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our ordered pairs in turn.\n\n- The ordered pair $({0},{1,0})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since ${0}⊆{1,0}.\\,\\times $\n\n- The ordered pair $({1,0},{0})$ is an element of $R$ since ${1,0}⊈{0}.\\,✓$\n\n- The ordered pair $({1,0},{1,0})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since ${1,0}⊆{1,0}.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411421 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80827" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411421 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411421 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X={-3,4}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $X?$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {X}^{2}\\,:\\,x>y-2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4d6f6f1-1f94-593d-9a86-a74c8d692551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411421 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $S$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {X}^{2}$ such that $x>y-2.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in X$ to see which elements $y\\in X$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=-3,$ then the value $y=-3$ satisfies $x>y-2.$ So, $S$ contains the ordered pair $(-3,-3).$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=-3,4$ satisfy $x>y-2.$ So, $S$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,-3),(4,4).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nS={(-3,-3),(4,-3),(4,4)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411422 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411422 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411422 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411422 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B={-1,0,1}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $B?$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,|x|\\ne |y|}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e334acc3-1257-55fa-a023-43a93b22b41c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411422 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ such that $|x|\\ne |y|.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in B$ to see which elements $y\\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=-1,$  then the value $y=0$ satisfies $|x|\\ne |y|.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(-1,0).$\n\n- If $x=0,$ then the values $y=-1$ and $y=1$ satisfy $|x|\\ne |y|.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(0,-1)$ and $(0,1).$\n\n- If $x=1,$  then the value $y=0$ satisfies $|x|\\ne |y|.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(-1,0),(0,-1),(0,1),(1,0)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411423 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411423 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411423 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,2}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $1\\,R\\,1$\n- $1\\,R\\,2$\n- $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411423 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3479154-8b81-59b7-8f1a-b3765342f61c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411423 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∣$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∤$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣$, we have $1∣1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $1∣2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $2∤2,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411424 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A={2,5}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $3$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e361298d-a398-53c4-959a-8c4c1fccbe8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411424 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(2-2)$ and $3∣(2-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2),(2,5).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(5-2)$ and $3∣(5-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(5,2),(5,5).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(2,5),(5,2),(5,5)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411425 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411425 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411425 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411425 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B={0,1,2,3,4}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $B?$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,y=2x}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411425 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8f12d3f-cce2-55c4-a847-238022b586e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411425 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ such that $y=2x.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in B$ to see which elements $y\\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=0,$  then the value $y=0$ satisfies $y=2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $y=2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair  $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $y=2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair  $(2,4).$\n\n- If $x=3,$  then there is no value of $y\\in B$ that satisfies $y=2x.$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then there is no value of $y\\in B$ that satisfies $y=2x.$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(0,0),(1,2),(2,4)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411426 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411426 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411426 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411426 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A={1,5,8,10}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $6$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411426 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea035c4b-8c95-55ec-bda8-6412f4cfc534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411426 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(1-1).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(5-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(5,5).$\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=8$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(8-8).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,8).$\n\n- If $x=10,$ then the value $y=10$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(10-10).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(10,10).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(5,5),(8,8),(10,10)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411427 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411427 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411427 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={e,\\pi }\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x\\ne y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $e\\,R\\,e$\n- $e\\,R\\,\\pi $\n- $\\pi \\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,\\pi $" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411427 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbdd486a-767d-532e-973f-5e22a4525b04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411427 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\ne y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\ne $ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne $, we have $e\\ne e,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $e\\ne \\pi ,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $\\pi =\\pi ,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411428 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411428 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212992" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411428 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411428 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A={2,6,7}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $3$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411428 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fde29981-bedc-59e2-aa69-f2b0b112afe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411428 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(2-2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=6$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(6-6).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,6).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=7$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(7-7).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,7).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(6,6),(7,7)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
